     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, again, <div><br></div><div>Is there any way to reduce the gap between plots more? The best I have been able to do is remove the margin on the plots. See below:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">ggtree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">cowplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># create random tree</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">set</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">seed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">123</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>tree </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rtree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">30</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># plot tree</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggtree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">tree</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> branch</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">length </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;none&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># plot same tree side by side</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>p1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> geom_tiplab</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hjust </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.5</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> offset </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.9</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggplot2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">theme</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">plot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">margin </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> margin</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> unit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;mm&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>p2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> scale_x_reverse</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ggplot2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">theme</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">plot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">margin </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> margin</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> unit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;mm&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>cowplot</span><span style=\"color:#660\">::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">plot_grid</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">p1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> p2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> ncol</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Also, I am unable to plot pie charts on the nodes of the reversed tree. </div><div><br></div><div>For example the code below results in the attached tree</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#800\"># make data for pie charts</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>A </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> runif</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">29</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>B </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A <br>data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">frame</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">node</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> A</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>data<br><br><br>pies </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nodepie</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> cols </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br>inset</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">p2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> pies</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div>Apologies if I have missed something simple!</div><span><div><br></div><div>Cheers</div><div>Ollie</div><div><br></div><br>On Monday, March 26, 2018 at 11:37:44 PM UTC+1, Ollie White wrote:</span><span><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello, <div><br></div><div>If you generated a tree using:</div><div><br></div><div><div>   library(ggtree)</div><div>   set.seed(123)<br></div><div>   tree &lt;- rtree(30)<br></div><div>   ggtree(tree, branch.length = &quot;none&quot;) + geom_tiplab()<br></div></div><div><br></div><div>Would it be possible to plot the same tree side by side, similar to the attached image?</div><div><br></div><div>I would like to map two different character states in the form of pie charts onto the nodes of the same tree, but when I have done this it looks quite cluttered. I thought presenting the results with the same tree side by side and different characters for each might be a better way of presenting this. </div><div><br></div><div>Hope this makes sense </div><div><br></div><div>Cheers</div><div>Ollie</div></div></blockquote></span></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
